"}],"deletes":[],"summary":"Added persistent redirect click events, database-backed analytics aggregation, self-origin and length validation, and Referer capture while preserving existing link and redirect contracts.","decisions":[{"title":"Preserve legacy constructors","rationale":"Existing unit tests or callers may instantiate UrlValidator and LinkService directly; compatibility overloads avoid breaking those APIs while Spring uses the injected production constructors.","alternatives":["Remove legacy constructors and require dependency injection everywhere","Use field injection"]},{"title":"Use native H2 aggregation queries","rationale":"The daily grouping and top-five limit are performed by the database as required, with explicit UTC timestamp bounds and deterministic ordering.","alternatives":["Load events into Java and aggregate","Use unbounded repository results and truncate in application code"]},{"title":"Keep click_count and click events together","rationale":"The redirect transaction writes the event and increments the existing legacy counter in the same transaction, preserving both response behavior and analytics consistency.","alternatives":["Replace click_count with event counts","Write analytics asynchronously"]},{"title":"Use server configuration for fallback origin","rationale":"The fallback uses configured server address, port, and SSL state and never reads request headers, preventing Host-header manipulation from affecting validation.","alternatives":["Derive origin from the incoming request","Use a fixed hard-coded public origin"]}]}

{
  "summary": "The change set contains a production-blocking startup failure, does not fully honor the fixed public-origin requirement, and allows oversized Referer headers to cause redirect failures. Analytics aggregation itself is database-backed, but the redirect and analytics endpoints have no abuse controls.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "CRITICAL",
      "file": "src/main/java/com/example/shortener/LinkService.java; src/main/java/com/example/shortener/AnalyticsService.java",
      "issue": "The @Autowired constructors require a java.time.Clock bean, but the application defines no Clock bean. Spring will fail application-context startup because no Clock dependency can be autowired, preventing the production service from starting.",
      "recommendation": "Register a singleton Clock bean, preferably Clock.systemUTC(), in application configuration, or remove the required Clock constructor dependency and use a safely injectable default. Ensure both services use the same configured UTC clock."
    },
    {
      "severity": "HIGH",
      "file": "src/main/java/com/example/shortener/LinkController.java",
      "issue": "buildShortUrl constructs returned short URLs from request.getScheme() and request.getServerName(), which are derived from the incoming Host header in common servlet deployments. An attacker can submit a request with a forged Host header and receive a poisoned shortUrl/Location value pointing at an attacker-controlled origin. This also violates the requirement that the public origin never be derived from the request Host header.",
      "recommendation": "Inject PublicOriginProvider (or an equivalent configured-origin value) into LinkController and build short URLs from that fixed origin plus the application context path and code. Do not use request scheme, server name, or Host-derived values for public URL generation."
    },
    {
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "file": "src/main/java/com/example/shortener/RedirectController.java; src/main/java/com/example/shortener/LinkService.java; src/main/java/com/example/shortener/ClickEvent.java",
      "issue": "The Referer header is accepted without an application-level size limit and persisted into a VARCHAR-like column limited to 2048 characters. A client can send a Referer longer than 2048 characters, causing persistence to fail during redirect processing and returning an error instead of the existing redirect response. Repeated requests can be used for error/availability abuse.",
      "recommendation": "Apply an explicit maximum length before persistence and define a safe behavior, such as rejecting the header value and storing null or a bounded representation while still completing the redirect. Configure a compatible server request-header limit as defense in depth."
    },
    {
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "file": "src/main/java/com/example/shortener/RedirectController.java; src/main/java/com/example/shortener/LinkController.java",
      "issue": "There is no rate limiting or abuse control on redirects, link creation, or the new analytics endpoint. Redirect requests can inflate click analytics, while analytics and link lookup requests can be automated for scraping and service-resource exhaustion. The 7-character code has a 42-bit space and is generated securely, so guessing is not trivial, but unlimited attempts remain possible.",
      "recommendation": "Add deployment-appropriate rate limiting and monitoring, at minimum per client/IP or trusted gateway identity, with separate limits for link creation, redirects, and analytics. Consider throttling repeated unknown-code requests."
    },
    {
      "severity": "LOW",
      "file": "src/main/java/com/example/shortener/PublicOriginProvider.java",
      "issue": "The configured app.public-origin is validated for scheme and host but is not required to be a pure origin. Values containing userinfo, path, query, or fragment are accepted and silently reduced to scheme/host/effective-port for comparisons, which can conceal configuration mistakes and produce behavior different from the configured public URL.",
      "recommendation": "Require an HTTP/HTTPS URI with no userinfo, path other than empty or '/', query, or fragment, and reject invalid origin configuration at startup with a clear configuration error."
    }
  ],
  "decisions": [
    {
      "title": "Treat the missing Clock bean as a release blocker",
      "rationale": "The explicitly selected @Autowired constructors require a dependency that is not provided anywhere in the application, so the application cannot start in its current production configuration.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Classify it as a medium configuration issue; rejected because it prevents all endpoints from serving.",
        "Assume a Clock bean is supplied by an external configuration module; rejected because no such module or bean is present in the supplied project."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Report request-derived short URL generation separately from self-origin validation",
      "rationale": "The validator correctly avoids using the request Host header for self-origin classification, but LinkController still uses request-derived origin data when constructing API responses. The requirement applies to the public origin generally, and this creates a concrete Host-header poisoning issue.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Do not report it because it preserves the pre-existing response behavior; rejected because the requirement explicitly mandates configured origin handling.",
        "Classify it as low severity only; rejected because consumers may trust and publish the returned shortUrl."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Report missing rate limiting despite it being out of scope in the functional specification",
      "rationale": "The security review instructions explicitly require checking missing rate limiting. It is therefore recorded as hardening/abuse exposure rather than treated as a functional acceptance failure.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Omit the finding because authentication and rate limiting are out of scope; rejected by the review instructions.",
        "Classify it as high severity; rejected because the supplied requirements explicitly exclude abuse-detection controls."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Do not report SQL injection in analytics queries",
      "rationale": "The queries use named parameters for link IDs and time bounds, and the fixed SQL fragments do not incorporate user-controlled code values. The database performs the required aggregation and limiting.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Report native SQL usage as inherently unsafe; rejected because parameterization prevents injection here.",
        "Require application-memory aggregation; rejected because it would violate the stated scalability requirement."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Do not report secrets in code or configuration",
      "rationale": "The supplied source and application configuration contain no credentials, API keys, or passwords.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Treat the H2 datasource URL as a secret; rejected because it contains no credential material."
      ]
    }
  ]
